A null pointer exception is a runtime error that occurs when an object is dereferenced (i.e., accessed through a pointer) and the object is not initialized or is not null. This can happen when a program attempts to access a null pointer, which is an object that has not been initialized or is not defined.

In other words, a null pointer exception is a type of error that occurs when a program tries to access a null or undefined object, which can lead to undefined behavior or crashes.

Here's an example in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null;
        System.out.println(str.length()); // This will throw a null pointer exception
    }
}
```

In this example, the `str` variable is declared as `null`, which means it has not been initialized or defined. When you try to access its `length()` method, you get a null pointer exception.

To avoid null pointer exceptions, you should always check if a variable is null before trying to access its properties or methods. You can also use null checks to prevent null pointer exceptions from occurring in the first place.
